  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_pDJzyPGWz2DfWTqZ",
   "name": "Radians & Angular Displacement",
   "definition": ""
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Radians & angular displacement"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Angles in radians"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **radian** (rad) is defined as:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**The angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc equal in length to the radius of the circle**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Visual definition of radian"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/05/6-1-1-one-radian_sl-physics-rn.png",
   "file": "assets/f37bf11a9390-6-1-1-one-radian-sl-physics-rn.png",
   "alt": "6-1-1-one-radian_sl-physics-rn",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***When the angle is equal to one radian, the length of the arc (S) is equal to the radius (r) of the circle***"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Radians are commonly written in terms of π\n- The angle in radians for a complete circle (360°) is equal to:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{circumference} \\mathrm{of} \\mathrm{circle}}{\\mathrm{radius}}=\\frac{2πr}{r}=2π$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Use the following equation to convert from degrees to radians:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`theta deg x ( pi / 180 ) = theta rad`"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**Table of common degrees to radians conversions**"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "md": "| **Degrees (°)** | **Radians (rads)** |\n|---|---|\n| 360 | $2π$ |\n| 270 | $\\frac{3π}{2}$ |\n| 180 | $π$ |\n| 90 | $\\frac{π}{2}$ |"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Angular displacement"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In circular motion, it is more convenient to measure angular displacement in units of radians rather than units of degrees\n- Angular displacement is defined as: **The change in angle, in radians, of a body as it rotates around a circle**\n- This can be summarised in equation form:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\Deltaθ=\\frac{\\mathrm{distance} \\mathrm{travelled} \\mathrm{around} \\mathrm{the} \\mathrm{circle}}{\\mathrm{radius} \\mathrm{of} \\mathrm{the} \\mathrm{circle}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - Δ*θ* = angular displacement, or angle of rotation (radians)\n  - *S* = length of the arc, or the distance travelled around the circle (m)\n  - *r* = radius of the circle (m)"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Note**: both distances must be measured in the same units, e.g. metres"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Visual representation of angular displacement equation"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/05/6-1-1-angle-in-radians_sl-physics-rn.png",
   "file": "assets/08c36eeac530-6-1-1-angle-in-radians-sl-physics-rn.png",
   "alt": "6-1-1-angle-in-radians_sl-physics-rn",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***An angle in radians, subtended at the centre of a circle, is the arc length divided by the radius of the circle***"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Convert the following angular displacement into degrees:\n\n![WE - Radians conversion question image, downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes](assets/0884786fbde4-we-radians-conversion-question-image.png)\n\n**Answer: **\n\n**Step 1: Rearrange the degrees to radians conversion equation**\n\n$\\mathrm{degrees}\\rightarrow\\mathrm{radians}\\Rightarrowθ^{\\circ}\\times\\frac{π}{180}=θ\\mathrm{rad}$\n\n$\\mathrm{radians}\\rightarrow\\mathrm{degrees}\\Rightarrowθ\\mathrm{rad}\\times\\frac{180}{π}=θ^{\\circ}$\n\n**Step 2: Substitute the values to calculate**\n\n$\\frac{π}{3}\\mathrm{rad}\\times\\frac{180}{π}=\\frac{180^{\\circ}}{3}=60^{\\circ}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- You will notice your calculator has a degree (Deg) and radians (Rad) mode\n- This is shown by the “D” or “R” highlighted at the top of the screen\n- Remember to make sure it’s in the right mode when using **trigonometric** functions (sin, cos, tan) depending on whether the answer is required in **degrees** or **radians**\n- It is extremely common for students to get the wrong answer (and lose marks) because their calculator is in the wrong mode - make sure this doesn’t happen to you!\n\n![Radians on calculator, downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes](assets/da824ddb0ed7-12-1-1-1-radians-on-calculator-1.png)"
  }
 ]
}